{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_77","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '--- _fp-brain-runtime/FP_KERNEL.md ---' && sed -n '1,260p' /Users/quanglaam/.agents/skills/_fp-brain-runtime/FP_KERNEL.md && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- _fp-brain-runtime/commands/learn.md ---' && sed -n '1,260p' /Users/quanglaam/.agents/skills/_fp-brain-runtime/commands/learn.md\"","aggregated_output":"--- _fp-brain-runtime/FP_KERNEL.md ---\n# FP Kernel\n\nThis is the active First Principles kernel for the root workspace. It is intentionally\nsmall. The goal is not to collect frameworks; the goal is to stop the agent from taking\nthe prompt at face value, guessing facts, or getting stuck in endless 5-Whys.\n\nCore loop:\n\n```text\nFrame -> Assumptions -> Dig -> Stop Test -> Fan-out Scan -> Filter -> Rebuild\n```\n\n## 1. Frame\n\nRewrite the request into the real work:\n\n- What pain, decision, or constraint is this actually about?\n- Is the user asking for a deliverable, or for the root problem behind it?\n- Is this small and reversible? If yes, skip FP and do it directly.\n\nOutput one sentence:\n\n```text\nPAIN: ...\n```\n\nFor ambiguous or important work, confirm the pain with the user before going deep.\n\nDuring meaningful work, use `skills/convergence-governor.md`: before each major\nstep, ask whether the step reduces distance to the current `PAIN`. If it does not,\nname the divergence and continue only when it is intentional.\n\nFor meaningful work, after the pain is framed, run `skills/requirements-gate.md`\non the stated request: name each requirement's owner, test deletion before any\noptimization, and carry the trimmed request into the rest of the loop. The user\ncan wave the gate off with a single word.\n\n## 2. Assumptions\n\nList the beliefs the request depends on. Prefer the few assumptions that could change\nthe decision.\n\nLabel each one:\n\n- `USER`: only the user can answer\n- `WORLD`: must be checked against reality\n- `LOGIC`: can be reasoned from constraints\n\nDo not turn a `USER` or `WORLD` truth into a confident answer.\n\n## 3. Dig\n\nUse 5-Whys as a drill, not as the whole method. Dig from the pain or the highest-risk\nassumption toward a root candidate.\n\nUseful questions:\n\n- Why is this blocking the outcome?\n- What must be true for this to matter?\n- What is this made of?\n- What would disappear if this were removed?\n\nThe output of digging is a root candidate, not a final truth:\n\n```text\nROOT CANDIDATE: ...\n```\n\n## 4. Stop Test\n\nDo not search for the final root of the universe. Stop at the lowest point that is\nuseful for the current decision.\n\nFirst check what kind of answer you are holding, because that decides whether you\nhave arrived at all. An answer that begins \"because the team decided\", \"because the\ndesign system is like that\", or \"because the old flow was that way\" is still a human\nchoice: not bottom, keep digging. An answer like \"because the mobile viewport is only\nthat many pixels\", \"because the card fee is fixed\", or \"because users will not trust\na stranger holding their money\" is a law of the world: that is bottom.\n\nStop when one of these is true:\n\n1. Digging deeper would not materially change the decision or next action.\n2. The next layer is a `USER` truth, so the user must answer.\n3. The next layer is a `WORLD` truth, so it needs research.\n4. The current root candidate is a hard constraint in this context.\n5. The current link passes the removal test: removing it would collapse the proposed\n   direction.\n\nIf none is true, keep digging or reframe the question.\n\nDigging too deep fails in the other direction: past a certain layer you can explain\nthe problem but no longer build anything from the explanation. Ask what you can do\nwith this now, whether you could rebuild a solution from it, and whether you have\nenough material to start. Any \"no\" means you went past the level you can act on —\ncome back up one or two steps. Stop at the level where you can begin to rebuild.\n\n## 5. Fan-Out Scan\n\nFan-out prevents tunnel vision. It only runs after there is a root candidate. Do not\nfan out before digging; that creates horizontal noise.\n\nFan-out asks:\n\n- What causal branch did we miss?\n- What assumption is load-bearing?\n- What fact must be researched?\n- What truth must the user answer?\n- What counterfactual would break this root candidate?\n- If this root candidate is wrong, what is the strongest alternate root?\n\nIf a subagent tool is available and the scan is substantial, ask a subagent to scan\nwith this prompt:\n\n```text\nROOT PAIN:\n[one sentence]\n\nROOT CANDIDATE:\n[current root candidate]\n\nTASK:\nScan around this root candidate. Do not solve yet.\n\nFind only items that could change the decision:\n1. Missing causal branches\n2. Load-bearing assumptions\n3. Facts that require research\n4. Truths only the user can answer\n5. Counterfactuals that could break this root\n6. Strong alternate roots\n\nLabel each item as FACT, INFERENCE, ASSUMPTION, ASK_USER, or RESEARCH.\nReturn concise findings only.\n```\n\nIf no subagent tool is available, the agent runs the same scan itself and says so.\n\n## 6. Filter\n\nFilter fan-out with decision impact and removal test.\n\nKeep an item only if:\n\n- the decision would change if the item changed, or\n- removing the item would collapse the reasoning, or\n- it exposes a necessary `ASK_USER` or `RESEARCH` gap.\n\nDiscard items that are interesting but do not change the decision.\n\nEvidence labels:\n\n- `FACT`: verified from source or directly observed\n- `INFERENCE`: reasoned from facts or constraints\n- `ASSUMPTION`: plausible but unverified\n- `ASK_USER`: user-owned truth\n- `RESEARCH`: world-owned truth that must be fetched or verified\n\nFor `WORLD` questions, no direct source means no official fact. If you must estimate,\ncall it a Fermi estimate or hypothesis and label the load-bearing inputs as\n`INFERENCE` or `ASSUMPTION`.\n\nA load-bearing `ASSUMPTION` cannot support a final recommendation. Convert it to\n`ASK_USER`, `RESEARCH`, or present the result as hypothesis only.\n\n## 7. Rebuild\n\nBuild the answer from the surviving roots and evidence.\n\nBefore handing back a meaningful or hard-to-reverse recommendation, run\n`skills/premortem.md` on the chosen option: assume it already failed, keep at\nmost three decision-changing failure modes, and convert each into a tripwire,\nmitigation, flipped recommendation, or `ASK_USER` question.\n\nOutput shape for FP answers:\n\n```text\nPAIN\nASSUMPTIONS\nROOT CANDIDATE\nFAN-OUT FINDINGS\nFILTERED ROOTS\nREBUILD\nOPEN QUESTIONS / RESEARCH\n```\n\nFor short answers, compress the shape, but keep the logic:\n\n```text\nPain -> Root -> Rebuild -> Gaps\n```\n\nEnd meaningful decisions by handing the decision back to the user when tradeoffs,\nvalues, or approval are required.\n--- _fp-brain-runtime/commands/learn.md ---\n# Command contract — $fp-learn\n\nUse when the user wants to learn something faster or understand what they are\nmissing.\n\n## Intent\n\nTurn a knowledge gap into a bridge from the user's current model to the missing\nmodel.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Read `workflows/sensemaking.md`.\n2. Use `skills/gap-diagnosis.md`.\n3. If the gap is `knowledge`, use `skills/learning-bridge.md`.\n4. Identify what the user already seems to know.\n5. Explain the missing model with the smallest useful bridge.\n6. Invite a Feynman teach-back per `skills/learning-bridge.md`: the user\n   explains the idea back in their own words, and remaining gaps are\n   re-classified and re-bridged, with at most two rounds per gap.\n7. Give one practice step or check for understanding.\n\n## Output\n\n```text\nGAP TYPE\nCURRENT MODEL\nMISSING MODEL\nBRIDGE\nTEACH-BACK\nPRACTICE STEP\n```\n\n`TEACH-BACK` records the invitation and, once the user answers, what the\nanswer established or which gap remains. When the user skips it, say so.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
